15 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Scientific illustration summarizing metabolic and immune pathway changes in human autopsy samples and a hamster model of Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome \(PCS\). Panel A features color-coded heatmaps illustrating pathway activation or suppression across human heart, lung, and kidney tissues. Panel B provides a schematic diagram of the hamster model, highlighting the tissues collected at acute \(3 days post-infection\) and post-acute \(31 days\) timepoints. Panel C presents a comprehensive heatmap of Normalized Enrichment Scores \(NES\) from GSEA, displaying transcriptomic shifts in mitochondrial, metabolic, and immune pathways. The matrix uses a blue-to-red color scale to visualize enrichment patterns across different tissues and stages.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 31 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Scientific illustration and transcriptomic analysis of skeletal muscle in hamster models and human patients. Panel A diagrams the collection of quadriceps and vastus lateralis tissues across acute and chronic timepoints. Panel B displays a heatmap of Normalized Enrichment Scores \(NES\) across mitochondrial, metabolic, and immune pathways, comparing SARS-CoV-2-infected hamsters to controls and PCS patients to individuals with Type IIb fiber atrophy. Panel C utilizes lollipop plots to visualize gene set enrichment for skeletal muscle function and pathology. The plots use color-coded markers to indicate the significance and direction of transcriptomic shifts, highlighting persistent mitochondrial impairment and muscle dysfunction across species.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Scientific illustration of the olfactory circuit and neuro-transcriptomic analysis in a hamster model of PCS. Panel A provides anatomical schematics of the mammalian head, highlighting the olfactory bulb, trigeminal ganglion, and olfactory epithelium. Panel B features a heatmap using a purple-to-green gradient to represent Normalized Enrichment Scores \(NES\), showing widespread mitochondrial stress and immune activation across these neural tissues. Panel C utilizes lollipop plots to summarize enrichment in neuronal function, synaptic signaling, and sensory perception pathways. The visualization uses color and marker size to denote the direction and statistical significance of transcriptomic shifts contributing to persistent sensory deficits in the olfactory system.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 33 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Scientific illustration and transcriptomic profiling of neuroimmune and metabolic dysregulation in hamster and human brain tissues. Panel A diagrams key regions studied, including the medial prefrontal cortex, frontal cortex, striatum, thalamus, cerebellum, and substantia nigra. Panel B features a heatmap using purple and green shading to compare Normalized Enrichment Scores \(NES\) across metabolic, immune, and mitochondrial pathways in both hamster models and human COVID-19 autopsy samples. Panel C utilizes lollipop plots with brain region icons to summarize enrichment in gene sets affecting cognition, behavior, and synapse function. The visualization highlights the specific vulnerability of the frontal cortex to persistent transcriptomic alterations during the post-acute phase.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 52 0 R /Alt (Scientific illustration and clinical analysis of human blood and serum following SARS-CoV-2 infection. Panel A diagrams the workflow for single-nucleus RNA sequencing \(snRNA-seq\) of PBMCs from mild and severe COVID-19 patients across early and late recovery phases \(up to 1 year\). Panel B features a heatmap displaying mitochondrial OXPHOS and immune pathway enrichment across diverse cell types. Panel C illustrates the serum metabolomics workflow, comparing acute COVID-19 cases to PCS and recovered groups. Panel D presents volcano plots showing log2 fold changes and statistical significance for glycolytic and TCA cycle metabolites, highlighting persistent metabolic shifts and mitochondrial dysfunction in patient serum up to 24 months post-infection.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 53 0 R /Alt (Scientific illustration of human serum proteomics and the divergent trajectories of COVID-19 recovery. Panel A diagrams the longitudinal collection of serum samples from healthy controls and patients at 1 month and 6 months post-infection. Panels B and C feature volcano plots highlighting significant protein expression shifts in the Post-COVID Syndrome \(PCS\) group versus the Recovered group at 6 months. Panels D through G present heatmaps of normalized expression for proteins involved in the stress response, antioxidant defenses, OXPHOS, and glycolysis. The visualization uses red-to-blue gradients to illustrate how specific proteomic signatures distinguish a pathogenic PCS trajectory from successful recovery, emphasizing persistent systemic metabolic and mitochondrial dysregulation.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 54 0 R /Alt (Conceptual model illustrating the hypothesized link between mitochondrial health and COVID-19 recovery trajectories. The schematic displays three interconnected pathways: a successful recovery cycle where tissue repair is supported by the restoration of mitochondrial homeostasis; a pathogenic trajectory leading to Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome \(PCS\), characterized by incomplete mitochondrial recovery and impaired tissue repair; and a persistent PCS state. In this state, a feed-forward loop is established where chronic mitochondrial dysfunction reinforces sustained immune activation and systemic inflammation. The model summarizes how convergent multi-omics signatures across diverse tissues and blood samples point to mitochondrial failure as a primary driver of prolonged post-acute symptoms. ) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
